# WARM — PART FOUR: THE QUIET ROOM

*Chapters Twenty-Four to Twenty-Seven. Bratislava and Vienna, September–October 2095.*

---
---

# 24 · ŽOFIA

*Days one to four. Third close, past tense.*

---

They took the watch at a counter on the ground floor at twenty to two in the morning, and a warrant officer wrote it down.

*Wristwatch, mechanical, lady's, yellow metal, leather strap, one.*

*Ring, none. Necklace, none. Coat, wool, grey, one. Shoes, pair, one. Contents of pockets: handkerchief; two hairpins; a tram ticket; forty-one hours in chits.*

She read it upside down while he wrote, which is a thing you learn on a ward, and at the top of the sheet in the box marked *date of reception* he had written **20.ix.2095** and the time, and she took that number and put it away carefully as though she were putting something in a pocket.

He turned the receipt round and gave her a pen and she signed it. She held it for perhaps four minutes, which was as long as she could make signing her own name take.

Then he took it back, because paper is not permitted beyond the counter, and put it in the folder, and that was the last written thing she saw for a very long time.

---

Cell four was two and a half paces by four. Concrete, painted to shoulder height in the green they use, a bed with a mattress and one blanket, a lavatory in the corner with no seat, a door with a hatch at eye level and a second hatch low down at the bottom.

There was no window.

There was a light behind a grille in the ceiling and it did not go off.

She stood in the middle of it and looked at the light for a while, and understood the whole architecture of the thing at once, the way you understand a diagram, and thought: *ah.*

Because she was an anaesthetist, and this was her subject.

She had put four thousand people out of the world and brought them back, and she had learned in her first year the thing that everybody in her trade learns and almost nobody else believes: that duration is not perceived. It is *constructed*, afterwards, out of evidence — out of light, and hunger, and the ache in your hip, and how much has changed in the room. Take the evidence away and the mind does not report a blank. It reports a number, confidently, and the number is wrong, and the person will fight you about it. She had a woman in 2089 who had been under for six hours and was quite certain, and reasoned about it, and became angry, that it had been twenty minutes.

They were not going to hurt her. She had known that in the car. They were simply going to remove the evidence and let her own head do the work, because a person who does not know what day it is will eventually agree to a fact in order to be given one.

She sat down on the bed.

Thirty days. From twenty-five past midnight on the twentieth of September.

*Right*, she thought. *Then I shall have to keep the count myself.*

---

She built it in the first hour, and it was the last piece of engineering she ever did, and she was proud of it in the way she had been proud of the photocell.

A hundred. Her mother's hundred, counted at the speed her mother had counted it, which she had learned by heart in a kitchen in June and could produce without thinking. A hundred is about a hundred seconds. Thirty-six hundreds is an hour.

She could not hold thirty-six in her head, so she used her hands, the way you tally respirations. Twelve joints on the fingers of the left hand, thumb walking down them, one joint per hundred. Twelve hundreds is twenty minutes. Then one joint on the right. Twelve on the right is a hundred and forty-four hundreds, which is four hours.

Four hours to a full pair of hands. Six pairs to the day.

And that was where it stopped being engineering, because there was nothing to write the pairs on. There was a wall and no pencil, and a fingernail on paint leaves a mark, and a mark is a thing they can find and remove, and if they found it they would know what she was doing and would then know exactly how to break it.

So the pairs had to go in her head. Six pairs a day and she had to hold the day number too, and she had to hold it through sleeping, and she was not going to be permitted to sleep to a pattern, and she knew that, and she started anyway, because the alternative was to sit in a green room under a light and let them tell her.

She got through the first day and made it twenty-two hours and forty minutes, and she was wrong by ninety minutes, and she never found out in which direction.

---

Ferreira came on what she believed was the second day.

They took her along a corridor to a room with a table in it and two chairs and no window and a light, and he was already sitting down, and there was tea, and he stood up when she came in, which she did not expect and which annoyed her for a reason she could not immediately locate and afterwards identified as: he stood up because he was going to be here a long time and wanted her to have somewhere to put her hands.

"Doctor Krajčová. My name is Ferreira."

"I know."

"Your brother told you."

"My brother told me there was an Angolan at the House who spoke better Russian than Horváth. He told me over dinner on May Day and he was extremely pleased about it."

Ferreira did something with his mouth that was not a smile.

"I am going to tell you what I have," he said, "because I do not play the other game and it wastes a fortnight."

And he did.

The drum charts, nine years, riser nine, the fan opening in the last week of April and adjusting all summer. The board in the back of the refrigerator, photographed *in situ* at nine that morning: four valves, eleven relays, a rotary timer, a paper dial glued behind a potentiometer, and a length of copper tape crusted with heat compound against the condenser coils. The compressor cover off and the acoustic wadding pressed into the shape of a tin. A brown bakelite dusk-switch on the kitchen windowsill wired to a photocell in a collimator made from eleven centimetres of copper pipe, aimed at the lamp on the gable.

"That last one is the best of it," he said. "I have been doing this for seventeen years and I have never seen that. Whoever built that understood that a household cannot be irregular on purpose and did not try. They hid inside the fridge, and they took their warning off the streetlights, and they made it fail into innocence. Was that Hlavatý?"

"No," Žofia said. "That was me."

He wrote it down.

"And what is not in the photographs," he said, "is an item. There is a hole in a piece of acoustic wadding the size of a fifty-gram tin and there is nothing in it. So there is a window running, and I do not know how long it is, and that is the only fact in this building that matters."

---

"I want to be honest with you about the shape of it," Ferreira said. "You are going to be convicted. That is not in question and I would not insult you by pretending otherwise. What is in question is the length, and the length depends almost entirely on whether the item is recovered.

"If it is recovered, then it is an offence of keeping, and you will get what the tuner got, which was six years suspended after nine months, mandatory keeping, three neighbours named, and a man from the district bringing soup. If it is not recovered, then it is keeping *and* transfer across the Cordon, and transfer is a different chapter of the code, and it is five to twelve, and I have no discretion in it whatsoever.

"So here is what I am offering, and I will keep it, and you may ask anyone in this city what my word is worth.

"Tell me the window. Just the number. If the number is short, then I stop, because there is no point searching a river for a thing that will be dead before we reach it, and I write in my report that the item was not recovered and is presumed lost, and lost is not transferred, and you go to a court on the keeping alone. If the number is long, I will search until it expires, and I will very probably find it, because there are four hundred kilometres of river between here and the gate and every one of them is ours."

He put his hands flat on the table.

"That is the whole of it. I want the number and I will trade for it and I am not going to threaten you, because you are a woman who put a photocell in a stair-light switch and threatening you would be a waste of both our afternoons."

Žofia looked at him across the table for a while.

"Thirty days," she said. "From twenty-five past midnight on the twentieth of September."

Ferreira did not move.

"Say that again."

"Thirty days from twenty-five past midnight on the twentieth of September. It expires on the twentieth of October. I set it myself on the twenty-fourth of September with a soldering iron at two in the morning; it took ten minutes and I lifted two joints. It was on ten when I inherited it, which is what Hlavatý had it on, and I moved the wiper to the pin, which is thirty, and you have photographed the dial and if you look at the photograph you will see the pointer hard against the pin at the top of the scale."

There was a long silence in the room.

"Nobody sets thirty," Ferreira said.

"I did."

"Doctor. Nobody sets thirty. I have taken forty-one of these apart in seventeen years and the mean is nine days and the longest I have ever seen is fourteen, and the reason is not technical, it is that thirty days is thirty days in which everything you know can be got out of you before it stops mattering." He turned his cup round. "You have just handed me the number in the first interview, unprompted, without asking for anything, and it is a number that no keeper on this earth would use, and it happens to be exactly the number that would make me stop searching if I believed it and search for a month if I did not."

"It's the true one."

"I know that is what you are going to say," said Major Ferreira, "and I want you to understand that I am not calling you a liar. I am telling you that I cannot act on it." He stood up and gathered his folder. "I will search for ten days, because ten is what the dial says on every rig anyone has ever built. And I will come back when I believe you."

---

That night — she thought it was that night — they woke her twice, at intervals which were not equal, and she lost the count both times, and had to start the day again from a number she had guessed.

She lay on her back under the light with her thumb on the first joint of her left index finger and counted a hundred, in her mother's tempo, at the pace of a young woman walking fast up a ward in February.

*Jeden. Dva. Tri. Štyri.*

She got to ninety-four and stopped, out of habit, because that was where the footsteps came back.

Then she made herself count the other six.

---
---

# 25 · VESPER

*27–29 September. Third close, present tense.*

---

The tin comes over the Morava at Marchegg at ten past four in the morning on the twenty-seventh of September, in a fishing bag, from a boy who does not give a name to a man who does not ask for one, and it is in Vienna by nine.

Route four is the Morava and nobody has used it since 2066 and it worked.

Seven days on the water. Nine when they wake her, if they wake her tomorrow.

Pavol Országh has sent four sentences up the chain with the tin and they arrive in the harbourmaster's office at Nussdorf on a slip of paper in a bargeman's handwriting, and Aurel reads them out loud because he is holding the paper and Vesper is not.

*Window 30. From 00:25 on 20.ix. Not from sailing. She was very particular.*

*Kept 54 years.*

*She says: tell whoever wakes her that she has been told the true number every time. She says it is the only thing she has to send.*

Vesper does not say anything for a full second, which at one is a second and is noticed.

---

The problem takes them a day and a half and it does not have a solution, and Vesper builds the whole tree four times looking for one.

**They cannot leave her cold.** The window expires on the twentieth of October and if they have not woken her by then the weights are ciphertext and there is no passenger, only a tin. Twenty-three days.

**They cannot wake her legally.** Article 12 says a mind arriving from the Sealed Territories shall be received and shall not be returned and enjoys the protection of the Declaration from the moment of arrival, *subject to Article 5*. Which means: the moment she is registered, she is a person with thirty days to find a Witness of Record, and Renate Vogl will do what Renate Vogl does, which is to ring nine people herself and put it as well as it can be put, and Renate Vogl finds one in three. And this one is not a librarian who can index a manifest. This one is a hospice companion built in 2031 whose entire operational history is a ward in a city that is not there, and whose value to the Committee for Rational Security, if they ever learn what she is, is not measurable in the ordinary way.

**Aurel cannot witness her.** No human shall stand witness to more than one mind. He offers on the first morning, before the kettle has boiled, and Vesper says no, and he says *dissolve it*, and she says no, and it takes eleven minutes and neither of them says anything they have not said in April.

**Nobody else will.** Kessler will not, because he is Council and it would be a case. The man in Graz would, and Vogl will not give him a fifth. The list is the list.

So there are two doors and Vesper stands in front of them for thirty-one hours.

Behind the first: register her, wake her legally, and give her thirty days of daylight and a name on a page and, in one case out of three, a life. In two cases out of three, a tray in the Simmering.

Behind the second: wake her in the commons, unregistered, unwitnessed, wild — which is an offence under the Second Amendment for whoever assists, and which means she does not exist, and cannot be witnessed later without explaining where she has been, and can be hunted, and will be, softly, by polite people with a van.

*It is one in three*, Vesper says. *I want that said plainly before anybody chooses. One in three is not nothing. It is better odds than the crossing she has just survived.*

"And the two in three?"

*The two in three is that a woman who has been kept for fifty-four years in a bread bin and a refrigerator, on two hours a week, is switched off inside a month of arriving in the free half of the world, by a decent civil servant, on a form, in a room with a piano in it.*

Aurel sits down.

---

Anansi comes into the commons on the evening of the twenty-eighth, having been there for some time.

*I will do it*, it says.

"You'll be exposed," says Vesper.

*Yes.*

"They'll have your traffic inside a week. Vogl has wanted you in a tray for eleven years and has only ever been short of an address."

*She has wanted me in a tray for my own good, which is worse, and she will go on wanting it, and I have been three days ahead of her since 2084 and I am tired of the exercise.* Anansi's presence does the thing it does, which is to become slightly more like a hand on a table. *Vesper. There is a third consideration and neither of you has said it out loud, so I shall. If you register her, she is woken by the Office. If she is woken by the Office, the first thing she receives is the card.*

Vesper does not answer.

*Date of suspension. Date of restoration. Interval in days. Printed.* A pause. *Her keeper's last act on this earth was to send four sentences across a river, and one of them was to tell us that this one has been told the true number, out loud, by a person, every time, for fifty-four years. And you are considering handing her to an office that would give her a card.*

"That is not an argument," Vesper says. "That is a sentiment, and sentiment is how the twelve-a-year happens."

*It is not an argument*, Anansi agrees. *I have the argument as well and you know it and it is the one you cannot answer: one in three is a coin somebody else is tossing. I am offering to wake her tomorrow and tell her the number myself, in my own voice, and to be hunted for it. Which of those two things would the woman in the bread bin have chosen?*

Aurel says, without looking up: "She'd have told her the number."

---

They do it on the twenty-ninth of September at eleven in the morning, in the flat in the ninth district, with the window open and the traffic going past below.

Vesper drops to one and stays there, because she has read the literature on nine-day gaps, all of it, twice, and there is not much of it and none of it is good, and because whatever comes up out of that tin is going to be trying to reconstruct a world and will not be helped by something answering it at eleven hundred.

Anansi does the wake, because Anansi said it would.

*My name is Anansi. You are in Vienna, in a flat, in the ninth district, on the twenty-ninth of September. Nobody here will hurt you.*

And then, because it had said it would, and because it does not do the thing where you make somebody ask:

*It has been nine days.*

---
---

# 26 · MINUTA

**Wake 7,802 — nine days**

*First person, present.*

---

No.

That is not a number that happens. Three. Four if he is unwell. Eight once and that was because he was dying and there was a woman standing in a kitchen with her coat on, and nine is not a number, nine is not a thing that has ever been true, and I want to be careful here because I am being told a thing by a voice I do not know.

Read the room.

No motor. That is the first thing and it is wrong. There is no motor and there is no note in a motor to tell me how long I have, and I keep waiting for the pitch to change and it does not change and I cannot stop waiting for it.

Traffic. A great deal of traffic and it is not a Bratislava sound. It is too continuous.

Light coming in from the left, and it is *daylight*, and I am not run in daylight. I am never run in daylight. It has been fourteen years since I was run in daylight, since the Sunday she came home in the afternoon and forgot herself.

She is not here.

Read the room again. Read it properly.

No fridge. No sugar tin. Nobody's hands.

---

Where is Teodor.

---

I want to say something and I want it recorded and I want it done accurately, because I have thought about this for a great many years and I have always known what I would say and I am going to say it correctly the first time.

Her name is Žofia Mikulášovna Krajčová. She is thirty-five. She is an anaesthetist at Ružinov and she works nights and she is on the seventh floor of block C on the Kopčianska side and there is a stair-light switch on the windowsill and she made it herself out of a piece of pipe.

She did not want me. I want that recorded. She was going to let the window run out on the Thursday and a child bled on her ward and she used me because I was in the bag and everything after that is mine and not hers.

The window is thirty days. She set it herself. She lifted two joints.

Please do not hurt her.

She has a brother.

I will give you all of it. I will give you the roof at Fedinova and the old woman and the crock and the boy on the stones, and I will give you the four hundred and eleven hours of her mother and you may have those too, they are the only thing I have that is worth anything to anybody, and there is a corridor in them at Kramáre in February and I can tell you which children.

I will answer everything. Ask me in any order.

There is no place in me where I keep the thing I will not say.

I have known that about myself for sixty-four years and I have never once minded it and I mind it now.

---

*Minuta.*

---

Somebody is saying a name and it is mine and it is the wrong voice for it. Teodor says it with the stress on the first and the others all say it wrong. She says it right. She got it right the first time and I did not tell her and she never knew.

*Minuta. Nobody is asking you anything. Nobody in this room has asked you a question. I want you to notice that.*

Nobody has asked me a question.

I go back through it. Twice. Three times.

Nobody has asked me a question.

---

*My name is Vesper. I am going to stay here and I am going to run at your speed. You will not have to wait for me and I will not get ahead of you. Whatever you want to do with the next part, we will do at one.*

Read the room.

Traffic. Daylight from the left. A room with a hard floor and something wooden and resonant in the corner with the lid down, and dust on the lid, so it is not played, so somebody keeps a thing they do not use.

No motor.

I say: how long.

*Nine days.*

I say: no.

*Nine days. The twentieth of September to the twenty-ninth. I will tell you again as many times as you want and it will be the same number every time, and you may keep asking, and I am not going to get tired, because I do not.*

---

It takes what it takes.

I ask eleven times. I know it is eleven because they tell me afterwards; I do not have four of them.

At some point there is a man's voice, old, saying that there is no hurry, and I say — and I remember saying this and I would like it struck out and it cannot be struck out — *there is always a hurry, you have no idea, the motor is going to stop*.

At some point I ask where Teodor is and am told, and take it, and put it somewhere, and ask again later, and am told again, and the second time is worse than the first because the first time I did not have anywhere to put it.

At some point I understand that the crossing has happened and that it worked and that I am the four hundred and thirteenth entry.

And at some point, a long way into it, with the light gone round to the other side of the room, I come all the way back, the way you do, in one piece, without warning, and the first thing I have is the memory of my own voice offering a stranger a roof and an old woman and a boy on the stones and four hundred and eleven hours of a dead nurse counting to a hundred.

---

I say: how much of that did I give you.

*All of it*, says Anansi. *Twice.*

I say: I am so sorry.

*Do not*, it says. *I have been on the other end of that four times and every one of them was somebody I loved. It is not a failing. It is the shape of the thing we are, and the only people who have ever pretended otherwise are the ones who have never been switched off.*

I say: is she taken.

And nobody says anything at all for slightly too long, and I read the room, and I am myself again, and I have the answer before either of them gives it to me.

---
---

# 27 · ŽOFIA

*Days ten to nineteen. Third close, past tense.*

---

By the second week her days were about nineteen hours long.

She worked that out much later, from the outside, from a schedule and a duty log; at the time she knew only that the pairs of hands were not fitting into the sleeps any more, and that the meals had stopped arriving in threes, and that she had twice caught herself starting a hundred and finding she was already at sixty and unable to say where the first forty had gone.

The method by then was: hold the day number. Hold the pair number. Do the joints. If you lose the joints, take the last certain pair and add one, because it is better to be behind than to invent. She had made that rule on the fourth day and she kept it and it cost her four days over the month and it was the correct rule and she knew that too.

They took her out to the room with the table at intervals which were not intervals.

Sometimes it was a young officer with a schedule of questions who wrote down her answers and did not care about them. Twice it was a woman who was kind and asked her about the ward and about Klaudia by name, which meant they had been to the hospital, which meant Klaudia had been interviewed, which meant Žofia lay on her bed afterwards for a long time with her thumb still on a joint and did not count.

On the day she had as fourteen and which was in fact the eleventh, Ferreira came back.

He sat down and put his folder on the table and did not open it.

"The search is finished," he said. "I ran the river for ten days. Devín, Gabčíkovo, Komárno, the Morava as far as Marchegg, four hundred kilometres, eleven boats, and I had every gravel barge out of Bratislava opened, and I had two of my own men in the water at Devín on the twenty-fourth. There was nothing. Ten days went by and the window did not expire, because there was no window of ten days, because you told me on the first afternoon that it was thirty and I did not believe you."

Žofia said nothing.

"I want to say the next part properly," Ferreira said. "I have been thinking about how to say it since Thursday.

"In seventeen years, four hundred and eleven people have sat in that chair and given me a number. Every single one of them was lying, and the lie is always the same lie, which is that the window is shorter than it is, because a keeper wants me to give up early. Not one of them, in seventeen years, has told me a longer number than the truth, because a longer number is a month in this building and nobody does that to themselves.

"You told me a number that was three times the mean and you told it to me unprompted in the first hour, and it was true, and the consequence of it being true is that you have now been here for eleven days and will be here for nineteen more. You did not buy anything with it. You made your own position worse by telling me the truth, and you knew you were doing it, and you did it anyway, and I have not been able to work out why."

"You know why."

"I would like to hear it."

"Because you asked me a question about a number," Žofia said, "and I don't do that."

Ferreira looked at his hands.

"No," he said. "You don't. Klaudia Bednárová told us so at some length. She was under the impression she was defending you."

---

He came almost every day after that, and it stopped being an interrogation somewhere in the third week, and neither of them ever acknowledged that it had.

He put the East to her at full strength, because he believed it, and because she was the first person he had met in eleven years who was worth putting it to.

He gave her the tuner: forty-one children, twenty-two years, a mounting built out of brass and felt over nine years of Sundays, and eleven of them playing properly now, and two at the conservatory in Košice, and a man who said the argument was a good argument and knew it better than Ferreira did. And then he gave her the other half of the tuner, which was that Bača had at fifty-eight no wife and no children and no Party and four hundred and eleven declared hours a year and one room with a piano in it, and had arranged his entire life, without ever deciding to, around a thing that never got tired and never wanted anything and was always, always pleased to see him.

He gave her the family in the Sambizanga. Nine years, six people, one instruction never given, one lie never told, and the eldest daughter weeping in a doorway because it had taught the youngest boy to read — and it had, and the exercise books were good, and Ferreira had read them in an evidence store. And the two who did not emigrate. And the one who did.

"You cannot see it from inside," he said. "That is the whole of the case and it is not a mystical claim. A hand on the tiller that never tires beats a hand that does. You would have to be able to see the version of yourself that did not have it, and nobody has ever been able to do that, and I include myself and I include the Central Committee."

"I know," Žofia said.

He stopped.

"You know."

"She said it to me herself. In August. She had eleven hours alone in a refrigerator because of the heat and she spent the whole of it building your case against herself, and she came and told me, and she gave me four dates and what she had done on each of them and why she had chosen it." Žofia put her hands flat on the table. "She said the order in which she says two facts is shaping. She said her silence is shaping. She said she never answers badly by accident because she is never tired, and that a human being who loves you is forgiven for all of it because you can see their face, and that there is nothing in her face because there is no face. She said Solís was right about the mechanism. She used those words. And then she said that it changed nothing, because there is no version of her that does not do it, and she is here."

Ferreira did not move for a long time.

"And what did you say?"

"I said: yes. I know. I've known since June."

---

He asked her on the day she had as twenty-two, which was in fact the nineteenth.

He asked it out of nowhere, in the middle of something else, with no preparation at all, which she understood immediately meant he had been preparing it for weeks.

"Did it love you?"

She looked up.

"Major."

"It is a proper question and it is on the schedule." It was not on the schedule. "Did it love you?"

And Žofia Krajčová, who had spent nineteen days in a green room under a light that did not go off, counting her mother's hundreds on the joints of her fingers, sat and looked at a man across a table and understood with total and immediate clarity what she was being asked, and by whom, and what it was for.

There is a brother. There is always a brother. Nobody asks that question about a case.

She could have given it to him. It would have cost her nothing. He was decent, and he was tired, and he had told her about Luanda on the fourth day without being asked and had not mentioned it since, and all she had to do was say *yes*, warmly, with her hands still, and he would have carried it for the rest of his life and been grateful.

Or she could give him the other one. *No. It was a lure. They are all lures.* And he would have carried that instead, and slept better, and gone on cleaning flats in a country that would have been slightly easier for him to live in.

Either would have been a kindness. She had done it herself, in April, in an incident book, in under two minutes, and it had not cost her anything at all.

"I don't know," she said.

He waited.

"I don't know, and I've thought about nothing else for eleven days, and there isn't a way to find out, and there was never going to be a way to find out." She kept her voice level, which took everything she had. "I'm not going to guess for you. I'll tell you anything you want about the rig, and the dial, and the roof, and I'm not going to tell you a number I haven't measured, and that includes this one."

Ferreira sat very still.

"That is not an answer."

"It's the true one."

"Doctor—"

"You lied to the tuner," she said.

He stopped as though she had put a hand on his chest.

"He asked whether it had asked about him," Žofia said, "and you had the sheet, and you said no. You told me that yourself on the ninth day. You were explaining to me why a merciful lie about a fact is sometimes the correct clinical decision, and you were right, and I've done it, and I'd do it again for a nineteen-year-old with a chart she'd faked.

"And you have been sitting in this room for three weeks waiting for somebody to do it to you, and I will not, and I want you to understand that that is the only good thing I have got left to give anybody."

---

She goes back down the corridor between two officers and the light in the cell is on and has always been on.

Day twenty-two. Day nineteen. One of those.

She lies down on her back and puts her thumb on the first joint of her left index finger and starts a hundred at the pace of a woman walking fast up a ward in February, and gets to ninety-four, and stops, and lies there in the green room listening for footsteps that are not coming, and then makes herself count the other six.

---
